  "explanation": "The proposed research outlines a clear and pragmatic token-space intervention framework for multi-agent scientific ideation running on a consumer GPU. By avoiding hidden-state extraction and utilizing pre-computed snippet embeddings, the architecture respects the tight hardware limits of a single 12 GB GPU and a 6-week academic timeline.",
  "problems": [
    "Running multi-turn dialogues with an 8B quantized model while coordinating external retrieval can lead to high latency and context window management challenges.",
    "Relying entirely on rule-based textual facet overlaps for critique may lack the semantic depth required to properly guide novelty improvements away from medium-novelty bias."
  ],
  "improvements": [
    "Implement aggressive KV-caching and concise prompt templates to prevent context overflow during multi-turn agent loops.",
    "Incorporate lightweight embedding-based semantic similarity checks alongside rule-based textual facet overlaps to improve critique quality without weight tuning."
  ],
  "evidence": [
    {
      "passage_id": "s_2b16063eb98f16264625",
      "claim": "Neural language models can be enhanced to generate novel scientific directions grounded in literature.",
      "relation": "supports"
    },
    {
      "passage_id": "s_36f29447814fff82a153",
      "claim": "Iterative improvement can be enabled through an agent generating ideas and another agent critiquing them.",
      "relation": "supports"
    }
  ],
  "uncertainty": "The primary uncertainty lies in whether an 8B base model governed solely by token-space prompts can reliably escape the medium-novelty trap without the latent-space probing mechanisms used in prior work."
}
